rn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.3 years ago; Identity Crisis: Preserving Identiﬁability in Linear ... models, which includes the Fin, Wing, Nemo, and Tweety-Bird models, and prove that ... We also prove that a conjecture\nIdentity Crisis: Preserving Identiﬁability in Linear\nCompartment Models by Removing Leaks, Moving\nOutputs, and in New Models\nSeth Gerberding∗1\n1University of South Dakota\nJuly 26, 2019\nAbstract\nIn this work, we address linear compartment model identiﬁability, and prove several\ncases where changing the model in preserves identiﬁability. We introduce a new family of\nmodels, which includes the Fin, Wing, Nemo, and Tweety-Bird models, and prove that\neach of these models is generically locally identiﬁable. We also prove that a conjecture\nof Gross, Meshkat, and Shiu (2019) holds for cycle, catenary, and mammillary models.\nLastly, we examine a new kind of operation, moving the output, and show that it\npreserves identiﬁability in cycle models. Our proofs are aided by results on elementary\nsymmetric polynomials and the theory of linear algebra for input-output equations of\nlinear compartment models.\n1\nIntroduction\nLinear compartment models have become a staple in certain biological ﬁelds, including phar-\nmacology, ecology, and cell biology. These models describe how something, whether it be\ndrug concentration or toxins, move within a system. In this work, we focus on the identiﬁa-\nbility of linear compartment models. Identiﬁability is the ability to recover ﬂow parameters\nfrom a limited data set. This property is valuable for many reasons. Theoretically, the goal\nis to ﬁnd simpler methods to identify parameters in a model. But, before the how, we must\nanswer can: that is, before answering how to identify the parameters, we must answer if we\ncan. The purpose of this work is answer when we can.\nPrevious research has investigated some operations which may or may not preserve iden-\ntiﬁablity.\nThat is, given a model that is identiﬁable, does changing the model in some\n∗Email: seth.gerberding@coyotes.usd.edu\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRamsey robustness conjecture for Ramsey-infinite graphs — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/344331/ramsey-robustness-conjecture-for-ramsey-infinite-graphs)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Ramsey robustness conjecture for Ramsey-infinite graphs\n\nRamsey robustness conjecture for Ramsey-infinite graphs\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $r\\geq 2$r≥2, let $H$H be a graph, and let $\\mathcal{M}_r(H)$M r​(H) denote the family of $r$r-Ramsey-minimal graphs for $H$H. Let $\\beta_r(H;G)$β r​(H;G) be the Ramsey robustness of $G$G with respect to $H$H. A graph $H$H is $r$r-Ramsey-infinite when $\\mathcal{M}_r(H)$M r​(H) is infinite. Ramsey robustness conjecture. If $H$H is $r$r-Ramsey-infinite, then\n\n$$\\inf\\{\\beta_r(H;G):G\\in\\mathcal{M}_r(H)\\}=0.$$in f{β r​(H;G):G∈M r​(H)}=0.\n\nEquivalently, the only possible reason for robustness to be bounded away from zero over $\\mathcal{M}_r(H)$M r​(H) is that $H$H is $r$r-Ramsey-finite. The conjecture is motivated by constructions with arbitrarily small robustness, but remains open in general.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Victor Souza, “Blowup Ramsey numbers”, arXiv:1910.13912 (2021).\n\ngraph-invariantsramsey-theory\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n\n* * *--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProceedings of the International Congress of Mathematicians 2026 - Volume 6: Invited Lectures (Sections 12–14) | Recent Progress in Graph Theory Using Expansion (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/25M1807915)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In 1978, Erdős et al. [39] made the following conjecture for the Ramsey numbers of cycles versus complete graphs.\n[19].\n\nAs it nears the end of its first century, the study of Ramsey numbers of graphs has broadened into a rich and extensive area. Away from the central goal of improving bounds on Ramsey numbers for complete graphs 𝑅⁡(𝐾𝑠,𝐾𝑡), when one of the graphs 𝐺 or 𝐻 is sparse, then we may aim to give better bounds on 𝑅⁡(𝐺,𝐻). For example, a classic result of Chvátal et al. [23] states that if an 𝑛-vertex graph 𝐺 has bounded maximum degree, then 𝑅⁡(𝐺,𝐺) =𝑂⁡(𝑛), while much later, Lee [77] confirmed the Burr–Erdős conjecture by showing this holds more widely for graphs 𝐺 with bounded degeneracy.\n\nVery few Ramsey numbers are known exactly. The value of 𝑅⁡(𝐺,𝐺) has long been known when 𝐺 is a path due to Gerencsér and Gyárfás [48], when 𝐺 is a star (i.e., a graph with a single central vertex which is contained in every edge) due to Harary [52], and when 𝐺 is a cycle due to work by Bondy and Erdős [11], Faudree and Schelp [44], and Rosta [107]. More recently, Nikiforov and Rousseau [99] showed that equality in (9.1) holds when 𝐺 satisfies certain sparseness and separation conditions (see [99] for details).\n\nOpen in ViewerImageFig 9: Burr’s coloring of a complete graph on (𝑛 −1)⁢(𝜒⁡(𝐻) −1) +𝜎⁡(𝐻) vertices, which contains no red copy of the 𝑛-vertex connected graph 𝐺 or blue copy of the graph 𝐻. When 𝜒⁡(𝐻) =4, this consists of 4 disjoint red complete graphs, 3 with 𝑛 −1 vertices and 1 with 𝜎⁡(𝐻) −1 vertices, and all the other edges are blue.\n\nGiven these results, attention has turned to qualitative concerns. In 1978, Erdős et al. [39] made the following conjecture for the Ramsey numbers of cycles versus complete graphs.\n\nConjecture 9.1.\n\nIf 𝑛 ≥𝑚 ≥3, then 𝑅⁡(𝐶𝑛,𝐾𝑚) =(𝑛 −1)⁢(𝑚 −1) +1.\n\nNikiforov [98] showed that this conjecture holds if 𝑛 ≥4⁢𝑚 +2. Perhaps surprisingly, though Conjecture 9.1 remains open, it turns out to be true much more broadly when 𝑚 is large. Indeed, Keevash, Long, and Skokan [63] showed that 𝑅⁡(𝐶𝑛,𝐾𝑚) =(𝑛 −1)⁢(𝑚 −1) +1 holds if 𝑛 =Ω⁡(log⁡𝑚log⁡log⁡𝑚). For the Ramsey numbers of cycles 𝐺 versus graphs 𝐻 more generally, the following conjecture of Pokrovskiy and Sudakov [103] (often, but not always, stronger than a related conjecture of Allen, Brightwell, and Skokan [1]) is appealing.\n\nConjecture 9.2.\n\nThere exists 𝐶 >0 such that if 𝐺 is an 𝑛-vertex cycle and 𝑛 ≥𝐶⁢|𝐻|, then 𝑅⁡(𝐶𝑛,𝐻) =(𝜒⁡(𝐻) −1)⁢(𝑛 −1) +𝜎⁡(𝐻).\n\nA corresponding version of Conjecture 9.2 when 𝐺 is a path was shown by Pokrovskiy and Sudakov [102] and when 𝐺 is a bounded-degree tree by Montgomery, Pavez-Signé, and Yan [94]. The best result towards Conjecture 9.2 is one by Haslegrave et al. [53], who showed that it holds when 𝑛 ≥𝐶⁢|𝐻|⁢log4⁡𝜒⁡(𝐻). The proof in [53] uses sublinear expansion techniques to find a cycle of length 𝑛 when the graph of red edges satisfies certain expansion properties, using some ideas from the work of Liu and Montgomery [81] discussed above. Technical barriers from these methods seem to make it hard to make substantial improvements, and a proof of Conjecture 9.2 would be very interesting, as would be a proof of the remaining unknown cases of Conjecture 9.1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn exact robust Ramsey theorem for matchings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19851)\nciteturn4academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Keevash and Michaeli recently proved that, under the robustness assumption that \\(G\\) is an \\(s\\)-connector (i.e. \\(\\overline G\\) is \\(K_{s,s}\\)-free), \\(G\\) has essentially the same multicolour Ramsey matching properties as complete graphs, with an additive error \\(O(qs)\\), where \\(q\\) is the number of colours.\nTitle: An exact robust Ramsey theorem for matchings\nAuthors: Mengyuan Niu, Lanchao Wang\nDate: Thu Jun 18 07:00:39 2026\n\nKeevash and Michaeli recently proved that, under the robustness assumption that \\(G\\) is an \\(s\\)-connector (i.e. \\(\\overline G\\) is \\(K_{s,s}\\)-free), \\(G\\) has essentially the same multicolour Ramsey matching properties as complete graphs, with an additive error \\(O(qs)\\), where \\(q\\) is the number of colours. They asked whether the dependence on \\(q\\) can be removed. We answer this question in a stronger exact form. For \\({\\bf t}=(t_1,\\ldots,t_q)\\in\\mathbb N_+^q\\), let \\(R_s({\\bf t})\\) be the smallest integer \\(N\\) such that every \\(N\\)-vertex \\(s\\)-connector \\(G\\) satisfies \\( G\\to (t_1K_2,\\ldots,t_qK_2). \\) We determine the exact value \\[ R_s({\\bf t})=\\sum_{j\\in[q]}(t_j-1)+ \\max\\left\\{2s,\\ s+\\max_{j\\in[q]}t_j\\right\\}. \\] While Keevash and Michaeli's proof uses a compression algorithm based on the Gallai--Edmonds decomposition to reduce the colouring to a structured form, our proof is a direct minimal-counterexample argument together with a new counting method for monochromatic matchings which can be applied to \\(s\\)-connectors.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuestions — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/questions?tag=finite-geometries)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * ## Point-line incidence conjecture for even-cycle-free graphs\nopenOpen about 3 years · documented by 2023complex-representabilityfinite-geometriesmatroid-theory\n\n0 0 5\n\n  * ## Asymptotic bound for average plane-size in rank-4 complex-representable matroids\n\nLet $M$M be a simple $n$n-element, rank-$4$4, complex-representable matroid that is not the union of two lines. Its average plane-size is the average of $|P|$∣P∣ over the rank-$3$3 flats…\n\nopenOpen about 3 years · documented by 2023complex-representabilityfinite-geometriesmatroid-theory\n\n0 0 7\n\n  * ## Extremal bound for suboptimal $(2d+1)$(2d+1)-union antichains\n\nLet $V$V be an $n$n-dimensional vector space over the finite field with $q$q elements, and let $\\mathcal{L}(V)$L(V) denote its lattice of subspaces. A family…\n\nopenOpen about 4 years · documented by 2022antichainsextremal-combinatoricsfinite-geometries\n\n0 0 4\n\n  * ## Boros–Füredi–Kelly conjecture on skew lines in four-dimensional space\n\nLet $\\Sigma$Σ be a finite family of pairwise skew lines in $\\mathbb{R}^4$R 4. Suppose that the three-dimensional affine span (hull) containing any two of the lines contains at least tw…\n\nsolvedDocumented by 2020 · about 6 years agofinite-geometriesincidence-geometry\n\n0 0 9\n\n  * ## Point-line incidence conjecture for even-cycle-free graphs\n\nPoint-line incidence conjecture. If $G$G is $C_{2k}$C 2 k​-free, then\n\nopenOpen about 7 years · documented by 2019extremal-graph-theoryfinite-geometriesincidence-geometry\n\n0 0 4\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA very robust Ramsey theorem for matchings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.03139)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Title: A very robust Ramsey theorem for matchings ... We show, somewhat surprisingly, that the same is true under the rather weak robustness assumption that $G$ is an $s$-connector (i.e. $\\overline{G}$ is $K_{s,s}$-free) with $s=o(n)$.\nTitle: A very robust Ramsey theorem for matchings\nAuthors: Peter Keevash, Peleg Michaeli\nDate: Tue Mar  3 16:24:54 2026\n\nOur main result is a robust generalisation of the Cockayne-Lorimer theorem on the multicolour Ramsey number of matchings. It is moreover a generalisation of the transference generalisation of Cockayne-Lorimer, which (informally) says that the random graph $G \\sim G(n,p)$ with $np \\to \\infty$ has, with high probability, essentially the same Ramsey matching properties as the complete graph $K_n$. We show, somewhat surprisingly, that the same is true under the rather weak robustness assumption that $G$ is an $s$-connector (i.e. $\\overline{G}$ is $K_{s,s}$-free) with $s=o(n)$. Moreover, we show that such $G$ has only an additive $O(s)$ loss with respect to $K_n$ for monochromatic matchings, which is essentially sharp. Our proof adapts a compression algorithm based on Gallai-Edmonds decompositions that we developed previously for generalised Ramsey-Turán problems.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Notes on learnability and human languages (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228775356_Notes_on_learnability_and_human_languages)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Theorem 2 (Gold, 1967) The class L fin of ﬁnite languages is identiﬁable. ... fin and any text T for L. ... Conjecture 1 The class L co−1∪L co−2 is not identiﬁable.\nφ(T [i]).\n\n(8) φ identiﬁes T iﬀ φ converges on T,φ(T ) ↓,and content(T ) =L(φ(T )).\n\n(9) φ identiﬁes L iﬀ φ identiﬁes all texts for L.\n\n(10) φ identiﬁes a class of languages L iﬀ for all L∈L,φ identiﬁes L.\n\n(11) A class of languages L is identiﬁable iﬀ some learner identiﬁes L.\n\nSometimes, when the context makes our intention clear, we will call such a class learnable.\n\n2.2 First results\n\nTheorem 1 The classes L=∅and L={L}for any r.e. language L are identiﬁable.\n\n(12) The previous theorem needs the qualiﬁcation “r.e” (recursively enumerable) since we are assuming that\n\nlanguages are represented by grammars (or machines) that recursively enumerate them.\n\nTheorem 2 (Gold, 1967) The class L fin of ﬁnite languages is identiﬁable.\n\n6\n\nStabler - Lx 212 2003\n\nProof: Suppose that we have an enumeration of grammars for all the r.e. languages. Then deﬁne a learner φ e\n\nas follows: for any text T,φ e(T [i]) is the ﬁrst grammar G in the enumeration such that L(G) =content(T[i]).\n\nCor.2.5A). Totality guarantees that the texts will contain, at some ﬁnite point, values that distinguish any two functions that diﬀer; in\n\nL sv we have no such guarantee, because the relevant values might be undeﬁned.\n\n13\n\nStabler - Lx 212 2003\n\nOne learner for this class is the function that, at each point, conjectures that the ﬁrst k elements of Σ∗not\n\nseen so far are the ones excluded.\n\nThe previous results do not generalize to L co−fin, though. In fact, we can show:\n\nConjecture 1 The class L co−1∪L co−2 is not identiﬁable.\n\nThis claim is intuitive: at each point in a text, the learner has no way of knowing whether one or two elements\n\nare excluded. In particular, suppose a learner guessed correctly in some case that the language was deﬁned\n\nby ∗x. The problem is that if from there on the text were really one for the co-2 language ∗x, ∗y, the learner\n\nwould not see the error. To prove this, though, requires that we show that the learner would never notice the\n\nmistake, and that is slightly tricky. To show this, the re sult in the following section will be extremely useful.\n\n2.9 Exercises\n\nDo the following exercises from Osh erson, Weinstein, and Stob (1986b).\n\n1. Suppose that some particular r.e. language L is given. Show that the class of languages {L∪D|D∈L\n\nfin}\n\nis learnable.\n\n2. Let L={L, L}be a set of r.e. languages such that L⊂L. Prove that no self-monitoring learner identiﬁes\n\nL.\n\n3. We saw that L co−1 is identiﬁable. Show that no self-monitoring learner identiﬁes L co−1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnticoncentration in Ramsey graphs and a proof of the Erdős–McKay conjecture | Forum of Mathematics, Pi | Cambridge Core (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/forum-of-mathematics-pi/article/anticoncentration-in-ramsey-graphs-and-a-proof-of-the-erdosmckay-conjecture/7B656805DB270806C3B0C4C6FC273AB6)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Anticoncentration in Ramsey graphs and a proof of the Erdős–McKay conjecture ... This brings together two ongoing lines of research: the study of ‘random-like’ properties of Ramsey graphs and the study of small-ball probability for low-degree polynomials of independent random variables.\n\n# Anticoncentration in Ramsey graphs and a proof of the Erdős–McKay conjecture\n\nPart of: Graph theory Extremal combinatorics Combinatorial probability\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  24 August 2023\n\nMatthew Kwan ,\n\nAshwin Sah ,\n\nLisa Sauermann  and\n\nMehtaab Sawhney\n\nShow author details\n\n* * *\n\nMatthew Kwan*\n    Affiliation:\n\nInstitute of Science and Technology (IST) Austria\n\nAshwin Sah\n    Affiliation:\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA; E-mail: asah@mit.edu\n\nLisa Sauermann\n    Affiliation:\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA; E-mail: lsauerma@mit.edu\n\nMehtaab Sawhney\n    Affiliation:\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA; E-mail: msawhney@mit.edu\n\n*\n    \n\nE-mail: matthew.kwan@ist.ac.at\n\n* * *\n\nArticle contents\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Introduction\n  * Short deductions\n  * Proof discussion and outline\n  * Preliminaries\n  * Small-ball probability for quadratic polynomials of Gaussians\n  * Small-ball probability via characteristic functions\n  * Characteristic function estimates based on linear cancellation\n  * Characteristic function estimates based on quadratic cancellation\n  * Short interval control in the additively unstructured case\n  * Robust rank of Ramsey graphs\n  * Lemmas for products of Boolean slices\n  * Short interval control in the additively structured case\n  * Switchings for pointwise probability estimates\n  * Competing interest\n  * Financial support\n  * Footnotes\n  * References\n\n* * *\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn n-vertex graph is called C-Ramsey if it has no clique or independent set of size Image$C\\log _2 n$ (i.e., if it has near-optimal Ramsey behavior). \n* * *\n\n## MSC classification\n\nPrimary: 60C05: Combinatorial probability 05D10: Ramsey theory\n\nSecondary: 05C35: Extremal problems\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Discrete Mathematics\n\nInformation\n    \n\nForum of Mathematics, Pi , Volume 11 , 2023 , e21\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/fmp.2023.17 [Opens in a new window]\n\nCreative Commons\n    \n\nImage: Creative Common License - CCImage: Creative Common License - BY\n\nThis is an Open Access article, distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution licence (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted re-use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\n\nCopyright\n    \n\n© The Author(s), 2023. Published by Cambridge University Press\n\n* * *\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nAn induced subgraph of a graph is called homogeneous if it is a clique or independent set (i.e., all possible edges are present, or none are). One of the most fundamental results in Ramsey theory, proved in 1935 by Erdős and Szekeres [Reference Erdős and Szekeres38], states that every n-vertex graph contains a homogeneous subgraph with at least Image $\\frac {1}{2}\\log _{2}n$ vertices.Footnote ^{1} On the other hand, Erdős [Reference Erdős33] famously used the probabilistic method to prove that, for all Image $n\\ge 3$ , there is an n-vertex graph with no homogeneous subgraph on Image $2\\log _{2}n$ vertices. Despite significant effort (see, for example, [Reference Barak, Rao, Shaltiel and Wigderson11, Reference Li71, Reference Chattopadhyay and Zuckerman20, Reference Frankl and Wilson47, Reference Cohen24, Reference Chung21, Reference Nagy75, Reference Abbott1, Reference Frankl48, Reference Gopalan52]), there are no known nonprobabilistic constructions of graphs with comparably small homogeneous sets, and in fact the problem of explicitly constructing such graphs is intimately related to randomness extraction in theoretical computer science (see, for example, [Reference Shaltiel89] for an introduction to the topic).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdős Problems (https://www.erdosproblems.